### Calculating Mobility: Worked Examples {#sec:mobility-examples-10}

#### Example 1: Simple Four-Bar Linkage {-}


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Example 1 — Simple planar four-bar linkage (RRRR). Link 1 is the ground (hatched). Links 2–4 are moving links. Four revolute joints connect them in a closed loop.](images/linkage-ex1-fourbar-dof.jpg){#fig-ex1-fourbar-10 fig-align='center' width=70%}
:::
:::


::: {.question}
###### Problem Statement {-}

Determine the mobility of the planar four-bar linkage shown in Figure \@ref(fig:fig-ex1-fourbar-10). All joints are revolute (pin) joints.
:::

::: {.answer}
###### Solution {-}

**Step 1: Count links ($n$)**

| Link | Description |
|:-----|:------------|
| Link 1 | Ground (fixed frame) |
| Link 2 | Input crank |
| Link 3 | Coupler |
| Link 4 | Output rocker |

$$n = 4$$

**Step 2: Count full joints ($j_1$)**

There are 4 revolute (pin) joints — one at each corner of the four-bar loop.

$$j_1 = 4$$

**Step 3: Count half joints ($j_2$)**

No cam or gear pairs are present.

$$j_2 = 0$$

**Step 4: Apply Equation** \@ref(eq:gruebler-10)

$$M = 3(4 - 1) - 2(4) - 0 = 9 - 8 = \boxed{1}$$

**Conclusion:** The four-bar linkage has **M = 1** — it is a proper mechanism requiring exactly one input (e.g., a motor driving the crank).
